BODY = """
<h3>Why first-line and relapsed disease are together</h3>
<p>In most lymphomas the evidence divides naturally into first treatment and relapse. In Richter transformation it does not. No regimen has been established by a randomized trial at either point, most trials enroll untreated and previously treated patients together, and what is used at relapse is whatever was not used first. Results at relapse are poor with every approach: in the KEYNOTE-170 cohort, where 83% of patients had received two or more lines of therapy, median survival was 3.8 months [[KEYNOTE-170, Richter cohort (Armand)]]. This Perspective therefore follows the disease from diagnosis onward and notes, for each treatment, what is known in untreated and in relapsed patients.</p>

<h3>How often, and in whom</h3>
<p>In newly diagnosed CLL followed at one center, transformation to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma occurred in 2.3% of patients, about 0.5% per year, and 1% per year once CLL had been treated [[Mayo Clinic newly diagnosed cohort (Parikh)]]. Among 2,975 patients in the German first-line trials, 3% developed Richter transformation, and median survival afterwards was 9 months [[GCLLSG pooled analysis (Al-Sawaf)]]. The risk follows the biology of the CLL (unmutated IGHV, high-risk FISH abnormalities, stereotyped B-cell receptors) and its treatment: a purine analogue with an alkylating agent tripled it [[Mayo Clinic newly diagnosed cohort (Parikh)]].</p>
<p>Genomic studies have changed the picture of where the lymphoma comes from. Clonally related Richter transformation arises from the CLL clone by acquiring about 20 additional lesions, with loss of TP53 or CDKN2A in about half of cases, and its genome is distinct from that of de novo diffuse large B-cell lymphoma [[Genetic lesions of transformation (Fabbri)]] [[Two genetic pathways (Chigrinova)]]. Subclones with the features of Richter cells can be found at the diagnosis of CLL, up to 19 years before transformation, and they show low B-cell receptor signaling [[Early seeding of transformation (Nadeu)]]. A later study of 52 patients defined molecular subtypes and suggested that cell-free DNA might detect transformation early [[Evolutionary history of transformation (Parry)]]. None of this guides treatment yet, but it helps explain why drugs that work in CLL work poorly here.</p>

<h3>Diagnosis</h3>
<p>The diagnosis needs tissue. PET selects the site: in 332 patients, an SUVmax of 10 or more was associated with a median survival of 6.9 months against 56.7 months below 10, and fine-needle aspiration was inadequate in 53% of cases of Richter syndrome [[FDG-PET and histology (Falchi)]]. That threshold came from patients treated with chemoimmunotherapy. After ibrutinib or idelalisib, an SUVmax of 10 had a sensitivity of 71% and a specificity of 50%, and 5 of 6 patients who later transformed had started below it [[PET after BCR pathway inhibitors (Mato)]]. The ERIC consensus asks for PET-CT, an excisional biopsy of the most FDG-avid accessible lesion, and testing of the clonal relationship to the CLL [[ERIC consensus recommendations (Kittai)]].</p>

<h3>Prognosis: three questions</h3>
<p>The first prognostic score used five clinical factors: performance status, LDH, platelets, tumor size and number of prior therapies [[Richter score, MD Anderson (Tsimberidou)]]. Later work reduced the problem to three questions.</p>
<p>Is the lymphoma clonally related to the CLL? Unrelated cases had a median survival of 62.5 months against 14.2 months, and less TP53 disruption [[Genetics and clonal relationship (Rossi)]]. The EHA guidelines treat clonally unrelated disease as de novo diffuse large B-cell lymphoma [[EHA guidelines 2026 (Eichhorst)]].</p>
<p>Is TP53 disrupted? It was in 47.1% of cases, and its absence was associated with a 57% lower risk of death [[Genetics and clonal relationship (Rossi)]].</p>
<p>Has the CLL been treated? In 204 patients, median survival was 46.3 months when CLL had never been treated and 7.8 months when it had [[Mayo Clinic series of 204 patients (Wang)]]. The Spanish series combined prior therapy, TP53 alterations and a low platelet count: median survival was 75.3 months with none of these factors and 3 months with two or more [[GELLC real-world series (Abrisqueta)]]. The same holds after targeted therapy: among 309 patients who never received chemoimmunotherapy, median survival was 12.3 months when CLL had been treated, mostly with BTK or BCL2 inhibitors, against 59.6 months when it had not [[Transformation without prior chemoimmunotherapy (Kittai)]].</p>
<p>These three answers matter more than the choice among current regimens, and they should be known before comparing the results of single-arm trials, which differ widely in the proportion of untreated patients.</p>

<h3>Chemoimmunotherapy</h3>
<p>R-CHOP is the default by analogy with de novo lymphoma. In a prospective phase 2 trial, 15 patients with Richter transformation had a response rate of 67%, with a median PFS of 10 months and a median survival of 21 months [[R-CHOP, GCLLSG phase 2 (Langerbeins)]]. Replacing rituximab with ofatumumab and adding maintenance gave a response rate of 46%, a median PFS of 6.2 months and a median survival of 11.4 months [[CHOP-O, NCRI phase 2 (Eyre)]]. More intensive regimens did not help. Hyper-CVXD with rituximab had an early death rate of 18% in the first cycle [[Hyper-CVXD with rituximab (Tsimberidou)]]; the platinum-based OFAR regimen gave responses in 50% and, in its second study, a median survival of 6.6 months [[OFAR (Tsimberidou)]]; and first-line R-EPOCH gave a median PFS of 3.5 months, with 30% of patients dying without progression or response [[R-EPOCH, Ohio State (Rogers)]].</p>
<p>Adding venetoclax to dose-adjusted R-EPOCH produced complete responses in 13 of 26 patients, a median PFS of 10.1 months and a median survival of 19.6 months, with grade 3 or higher neutropenia in 65% [[VR-EPOCH (Davids)]]. In routine practice, venetoclax with R-CHOP gave complete responses in 46% of 13 patients, and venetoclax with a BTK inhibitor in 25% of 28 [[Venetoclax-based treatment, multicenter series (Hampel)]]. Among the chemotherapy-based treatments on this page these are the highest complete response rates, in small and selected groups.</p>

<h3>Transplantation</h3>
<p>The observation that patients transplanted in remission live longer goes back to 20 patients in the first large series [[Richter score, MD Anderson (Tsimberidou)]]. In the EBMT registry, 3-year survival was 36% after allogeneic and 59% after autologous transplantation, the latter mostly in chemosensitive disease [[EBMT transplant registry (Cwynarski)]]. In the CIBMTR registry, 3-year survival after allogeneic transplantation was 52%, and the depth of response decided the outcome: 77% in complete response, 57% in partial response and 15% with resistant disease, while del(17p) and prior targeted therapy made no difference [[CIBMTR transplant registry (Herrera)]]. With more than 6 years of follow-up, 3-year survival after allogeneic transplantation was 39% and nonrelapse mortality 38% [[EBMT allogeneic transplant series (Guièze)]].</p>
<p>All of these series count only patients who reached transplantation. With that limit, allogeneic transplantation in remission remains the best-documented way to long survival in clonally related disease, and the EHA guidelines advise considering it in eligible patients who respond [[EHA guidelines 2026 (Eichhorst)]].</p>

<h3>BTK inhibitors</h3>
<p>Acalabrutinib alone gave responses in 40.0% of 25 patients, with a median duration of 6.2 months and a median PFS of 3.2 months [[ACE-CL-001, Richter cohort (Eyre)]]. Pirtobrutinib gave responses in 50.0% of 82 patients, 90% of whom had already been treated for Richter transformation and 74% with a covalent BTK inhibitor; eight responders went on to transplantation [[BRUIN, Richter cohort (Wierda)]]. A small cohort treated with zanubrutinib, few of them previously exposed to a BTK inhibitor, had longer remissions [[Zanubrutinib, alone or with tislelizumab (Tam)]]. The reasonable reading is that a BTK inhibitor is a bridge and a partner in combinations, and not a treatment by itself, which is how the EHA guidelines place acalabrutinib and pirtobrutinib [[EHA guidelines 2026 (Eichhorst)]].</p>

<h3>Checkpoint inhibitors</h3>
<p>Pembrolizumab produced responses in 4 of 9 patients with Richter transformation and in none of 16 with CLL [[Pembrolizumab, Mayo Clinic phase 2 (Ding)]]. The confirmatory cohort was negative: 3 responses in 23 patients, two of them in the Hodgkin variant, and a median PFS of 1.6 months [[KEYNOTE-170, Richter cohort (Armand)]]. Combinations did better. Nivolumab with ibrutinib gave responses in 13 of 20 patients in one trial and in 10 of 24 in another, where responses lasted a median of 15 months [[Ibrutinib-nivolumab, phase 1/2a (Younes)]] [[Nivolumab-ibrutinib, MD Anderson phase 2 (Jain)]]. In RT1, tislelizumab with zanubrutinib gave a response rate of 58.3%, a median PFS of 10.0 months and a 12-month survival of 74.7%; only 20.8% of the patients had been treated for Richter transformation before [[RT1 (Al-Sawaf)]]. In MOLTO, a first-line combination of atezolizumab, venetoclax and obinutuzumab gave responses in 67.9% of 28 patients, without tumor lysis syndrome [[MOLTO (Tedeschi)]].</p>
<p>These chemotherapy-free regimens have response rates similar to R-CHOP with less toxicity, mostly as first treatment. They have not been compared with chemoimmunotherapy, and follow-up is short.</p>

<h3>Bispecific antibodies and CAR T cells</h3>
<p>The treatments that redirect T cells share a pattern: fewer than half of patients reach complete response, but those who do can stay in remission for years. In the international CAR T-cell series, 46% of 69 heavily pretreated patients reached complete response, and those responses lasted a median of 27.6 months, while median PFS for the whole group was 4.7 months and median survival 8.5 months; severe neurotoxicity occurred in 37% [[CAR T cells, international series (Kittai)]]. Epcoritamab gave responses in 47.6% of 42 patients, 57.1% in first line and 38.1% later [[EPCORE CLL-1, Richter cohort (Kater)]]. Mosunetuzumab in relapsed disease gave complete responses in 20% of 20 patients, with a median PFS of 3.4 months [[Mosunetuzumab, phase 1/2 cohort (Lewis)]]. With glofitamab, 5 of 11 refractory patients reached complete response, and 4 of them were still in remission at a median follow-up of 67.1 months after a fixed course, without transplantation [[Glofitamab, NP30179 Richter cohort (Carlo-Stella)]]. Blinatumomab was tested alone in 9 patients and then after two cycles of R-CHOP, where it converted 20% of patients not in complete response [[High-dose blinatumomab (Thompson)]] [[BLINART (Guièze)]].</p>
<p>How these treatments relate to transplantation is not settled. In the series of patients without prior chemoimmunotherapy, median survival was 54.5 months after allogeneic transplantation and 9.0 months after CAR T cells, but the two groups were not comparable [[Transformation without prior chemoimmunotherapy (Kittai)]].</p>

<h3>The Hodgkin variant</h3>
<p>Transformation to classical Hodgkin lymphoma is about one tenth as frequent, 0.05% per year, with a median survival of 3.9 years in the Mayo Clinic cohort [[Hodgkin transformation, Mayo Clinic (Parikh)]]. In 94 patients from a multicenter series, 2-year survival was 72%, patients given ABVD-based treatment had a median survival of 13.2 years, and transplantation in first remission added nothing [[Hodgkin transformation, multicenter series (Stephens)]]. Population-based data were less favorable overall, with a 2-year survival of 57%, but 74% in patients able to receive curative-intent ABVD [[Hodgkin variant, British Columbia (Zhu)]]. In the Spanish series, median survival was 30.8 months for the Hodgkin type against 5.9 months for the diffuse large B-cell type [[GELLC real-world series (Abrisqueta)]]. It is treated as classical Hodgkin lymphoma [[EHA guidelines 2026 (Eichhorst)]].</p>

<h3>Where we are now</h3>
<p>No drug is approved for Richter transformation, and no standard exists. The EHA guidelines give three graded recommendations for the diffuse large B-cell type: test the clonal relationship, offer a clinical trial whenever possible, and outside trials use chemoimmunotherapy, the last with the lowest grade of recommendation [[EHA guidelines 2026 (Eichhorst)]]. The ERIC consensus says the same about trials [[ERIC consensus recommendations (Kittai)]]. NCCN placements were not checked.</p>
<p>In practice the sequence is built from the pieces above. Clonally unrelated lymphoma, and to a large extent transformation in never-treated CLL, is treated as de novo diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. For clonally related disease after CLL therapy, a first treatment is chosen to obtain a response (a trial, R-CHOP with or without venetoclax, or a chemotherapy-free combination), and responders who are fit are considered for allogeneic transplantation. At relapse, the options are a noncovalent BTK inhibitor as a bridge, CAR T cells or a bispecific antibody, again preferably in a trial. This is an interpretation of single-arm data, and reasonable clinicians order these steps differently.</p>

<h3>Open questions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Chemotherapy or not:</b> do checkpoint, BTK and BCL2 inhibitor combinations match R-CHOP as first treatment? No comparison exists [[RT1 (Al-Sawaf)]] [[MOLTO (Tedeschi)]].</li>
<li><b>Consolidation:</b> after a complete response to a bispecific antibody or CAR T cells, is allogeneic transplantation still needed?</li>
<li><b>After targeted therapy:</b> most patients now transform during or after a BTK or BCL2 inhibitor, and their median survival is still about one year [[Transformation without prior chemoimmunotherapy (Kittai)]].</li>
<li><b>Diagnosis:</b> the PET threshold after targeted therapy, and whether cell-free DNA can replace or anticipate biopsy [[PET after BCR pathway inhibitors (Mato)]] [[Evolutionary history of transformation (Parry)]].</li>
<li><b>End points:</b> response rates in single-arm trials of 20 to 80 patients have not translated into survival gains; the ERIC consensus asks that trials prioritize survival [[ERIC consensus recommendations (Kittai)]].</li>
</ul>

<h3>What is coming</h3>
<p>The first randomized trials are under way (listed under Trials to watch): pirtobrutinib added to R-CHOP in a phase 3 trial (PIRAMID), pirtobrutinib with epcoritamab against R-CHOP (CLLRT2), and CHOP-R with or without acalabrutinib (STELLAR). Single-arm trials are adding glofitamab or mosunetuzumab to CHOP as first treatment (GLORIFY, GELLC9RICHTER) and sonrotoclax to tislelizumab and zanubrutinib (CLL-RT1). Early data on a BTK degrader and on epcoritamab combinations are under New drugs to watch.</p>
"""
